::GEO_U03_L01_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 5, 5, and 8. How is it classified by sides?
{
=A. Isosceles#Two sides match, so the triangle is isosceles.
~B. Scalene#Scalene means no side lengths match.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral requires all three side lengths to match.
~D. Right#Right classifies by angles, not side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 4, 6, and 9. How is it classified by sides?
{
~A. Isosceles#Isosceles requires at least two congruent sides.
=B. Scalene#All three side lengths are different, so the triangle is scalene.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral requires three congruent sides.
~D. Obtuse#Obtuse classifies by angles, not side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 7, 7, and 7. How is it classified by sides?
{
~A. Isosceles only#Equilateral triangles are isosceles too, but the most precise classification is equilateral.
~B. Scalene#Scalene means no congruent sides.
=C. Equilateral#All three sides are congruent, so the triangle is equilateral.
~D. Right#Right classification needs angle evidence.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 35°, 55°, and 90°. How is it classified by angles?
{
~A. Acute#Acute requires all angles less than 90°.
~B. Obtuse#Obtuse requires one angle greater than 90°.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral is a side classification and would have three 60° angles.
=D. Right#One angle is exactly 90°, so the triangle is right.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 40°, 65°, and 75°. How is it classified by angles?
{
=A. Acute#All angles are less than 90°, so the triangle is acute.
~B. Right#A right triangle needs one 90° angle.
~C. Obtuse#An obtuse triangle needs one angle greater than 90°.
~D. Scalene#Scalene classifies side lengths, not angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 25°, 35°, and 120°. How is it classified by angles?
{
~A. Acute#Acute requires all angles less than 90°.
=B. Obtuse#One angle is greater than 90°, so the triangle is obtuse.
~C. Right#Right requires one angle exactly 90°.
~D. Isosceles#Isosceles classifies side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which evidence is enough to classify a triangle as equilateral?
{
~A. The triangle looks balanced.#Appearance is not evidence for equilateral classification.
~B. Two angles look equal.#Two equal-looking angles do not prove all sides congruent.
=C. All three sides have matching congruence marks.#Three matching side marks show all sides are congruent.
~D. One angle is 90°.#A 90° angle identifies a right triangle, not an equilateral triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which phrase uses precise triangle notation?
{
~A. The triangle is kind of pointy.#This is informal and not mathematical.
~B. It is probably equal.#This is vague and unsupported.
~C. The shape looks right.#This relies on appearance and unclear language.
=D. Triangle ABC is acute scalene.#This names the triangle and gives precise classifications.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has all three sides congruent. What must also be true?
{
=A. It is equilateral.#Three congruent sides define an equilateral triangle.
~B. It is scalene.#Scalene means no sides are congruent.
~C. It is obtuse.#Equilateral triangles have 60° angles, not an obtuse angle.
~D. It has one right angle.#An equilateral triangle cannot have a right angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has exactly two sides marked congruent and one angle marked 100°. Which classification is supported?
{
~A. Scalene acute#The side marks show isosceles, not scalene, and 100° is not acute.
=B. Isosceles obtuse#Two congruent sides make it isosceles, and 100° makes it obtuse.
~C. Equilateral acute#Equilateral would require three congruent sides and three 60° angles.
~D. Right isosceles#A right triangle needs a 90° angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 6, 8, and 10 and one angle marked 90°. Which classification is supported?
{
~A. Isosceles right#Isosceles would require two equal side lengths.
~B. Equilateral right#Equilateral triangles cannot have a right angle.
=C. Scalene right#All side lengths differ and one angle is 90°.
~D. Scalene obtuse#A 90° angle makes it right, not obtuse.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 60°, 60°, and 60°. Which angle classification is correct?
{
~A. Right#No angle is 90°.
~B. Obtuse#No angle is greater than 90°.
~C. Scalene#Scalene is a side classification.
=D. Acute#Each angle is less than 90°, so the triangle is acute.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which statement is supported by two matching side tick marks?
{
=A. The triangle has at least two congruent sides.#Two matching side marks support at least two congruent sides.
~B. The triangle has three congruent sides.#Three congruent sides would need three matching side marks.
~C. The triangle is right.#Side tick marks do not prove a 90° angle.
~D. The triangle is obtuse.#Side tick marks do not prove an obtuse angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has no matching side marks and side lengths 3, 4, and 5. What side classification is supported?
{
~A. Isosceles#Isosceles requires two equal side lengths.
=B. Scalene#The side lengths are all different, so it is scalene.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral requires all three sides equal.
~D. Acute#Acute requires angle information.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which classification uses angle measures only?
{
~A. Equilateral#Equilateral describes side lengths.
~B. Isosceles#Isosceles describes side lengths.
=C. Obtuse#Obtuse describes a triangle with one angle greater than 90°.
~D. Scalene#Scalene describes side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which classification uses side lengths only?
{
~A. Right#Right describes angle measure.
~B. Acute#Acute describes angle measure.
~C. Obtuse#Obtuse describes angle measure.
=D. Scalene#Scalene describes a triangle with no congruent sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 9, 9, and 12. Which side classification is most precise?
{
=A. Isosceles#Exactly two side lengths match, so isosceles is most precise.
~B. Equilateral#Equilateral requires three equal sides.
~C. Scalene#Scalene requires no equal sides.
~D. Right#Right cannot be determined from these side labels alone in this readiness item.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 89°, 45°, and 46°. Which angle classification is correct?
{
~A. Right#No angle is exactly 90°.
=B. Acute#All three angles are less than 90°.
~C. Obtuse#No angle is greater than 90°.
~D. Equilateral#Equilateral is based on sides and would have 60° angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has one angle labeled 91°. What angle classification is already determined?
{
~A. Right#Right requires exactly 90°.
~B. Acute#Acute requires all angles less than 90°.
=C. Obtuse#One angle greater than 90° makes the triangle obtuse.
~D. Equiangular#Equiangular would require all angles congruent.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which evidence is not enough to classify by sides?
{
~A. Two sides are both labeled 8 cm.#Equal measurements support an isosceles classification.
~B. All three sides have matching tick marks.#Matching tick marks support equilateral classification.
~C. The side lengths are 4, 5, and 6.#Three different side lengths support scalene classification.
=D. The triangle appears symmetric.#Appearance alone is not enough evidence for side classification.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q21
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Triangle DEF has DE ≅ EF. Which side classification is supported?
{
=A. Isosceles#At least two congruent sides support isosceles.
~B. Scalene#Scalene means no congruent sides.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral would require DF to be congruent too.
~D. Obtuse#Obtuse is an angle classification.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q22
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle is classified as right isosceles. What evidence should be shown?
{
~A. Three congruent sides only.#Three congruent sides describes equilateral, not right isosceles.
=B. One 90° angle and two congruent sides.#Right isosceles combines one right angle with two congruent sides.
~C. One obtuse angle and no congruent sides.#That describes obtuse scalene.
~D. All angles less than 90° and no side marks.#That describes acute scalene.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q23
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which classification is impossible for one triangle?
{
~A. Acute scalene#Acute scalene is possible.
~B. Obtuse isosceles#Obtuse isosceles is possible.
=C. Right obtuse#A triangle cannot have both a 90° angle and an angle greater than 90°.
~D. Right scalene#Right scalene is possible.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q24
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which description supports an acute isosceles triangle?
{
~A. Three different sides and one 90° angle.#That describes a right scalene triangle.
~B. Two congruent sides and one 120° angle.#That describes an obtuse isosceles triangle.
~C. Three congruent sides and one 100° angle.#An equilateral triangle cannot have a 100° angle.
=D. Two congruent sides and all angles less than 90°.#This gives the side and angle evidence for acute isosceles.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_Q25
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Why should triangle classification not rely only on appearance?
{
=A. Drawings may not be to scale, so classifications need markings, measures, or given facts.#Correct. Diagram appearance can mislead, so evidence is required.
~B. All drawings are automatically accurate.#Diagrams are not assumed accurate unless information is given.
~C. Appearance is stronger than measurements.#Measurements and markings are stronger than appearance.
~D. Only color determines triangle type.#Color is not a geometric classification property.
}

